/* Exam 1 extension: instructor Lectures 10–16. Learning content remains pathology-first;
   future Clin Path links should point to common mechanisms rather than duplicating cards. */
Object.assign(pages,{
 edema:{title:'10 · Edema, Congestion & Hyperemia',source:'Instructor Lecture 10 · 39 slides',summary:'FOUR critical edema mechanisms; Starling forces, effusions, congestion versus hyperemia.',sections:[
 ['Starling forces: push vs pull','Hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out of capillaries; plasma oncotic pressure (predominantly albumin) opposes filtration. Lymphatics return filtered interstitial fluid. When pressure, proteins, permeability or drainage change, excess fluid accumulates.'],
 ['Mechanism 1 — INCREASED microvascular permeability','Inflammation or endothelial damage widens the barrier and lets water AND proteins escape. Gross edema/effusion may be protein-rich; microscopic edema may be pale eosinophilic. Contrast pure hydrostatic edema with no major endothelial injury.'],
 ['Mechanism 2 — INCREASED intravascular hydrostatic pressure','Venous obstruction or failure of the heart to drain the vascular bed causes pressure to back up; filtration of fluid increases despite relatively intact endothelium. Left-sided heart disease commonly leads to pulmonary edema. Right-sided venous congestion can cause dependent edema or cavity effusion.'],
 ['Mechanism 3 — DECREASED oncotic pressure','Reduced albumin production or excess protein loss lowers intravascular pulling pressure. Consider hepatic synthetic dysfunction, renal protein loss, gastrointestinal protein loss and severe malnutrition. Identify the mechanism before assuming an etiology.'],
 ['Mechanism 4 — DECREASED lymphatic drainage','Obstructed or damaged lymphatic channels cannot return interstitial fluid to circulation. Examples include lymphatic compression/obstruction, fibrosis or surgical disruption; look for localized or regional edema.'],
 ['Edema vs effusion','Edema is excess fluid in interstitial tissue; effusion collects freely within a cavity. Hydrothorax (pleura), hydropericardium (pericardium), hydroperitoneum/ascites (abdomen); anasarca = severe generalized subcutaneous edema.'],
 ['Fluid appearance and morphology','Pitting, gravity-dependent, cool swelling and clear to yellow fluid favor noninflammatory edema. Protein-poor transudate is often clear; modified transudate is intermediate; inflammatory exudate is protein/cell rich and may look opaque. Appearance alone does not replace fluid analysis.'],
 ['Hyperemia vs congestion','Hyperemia is ACTIVE increased arteriolar inflow (red, oxygenated, e.g. inflammation/exercise). Congestion is PASSIVE reduced venous outflow (dark-red/blue, often accompanied by edema); chronic hepatic congestion can create a mottled pattern.'],
 ['Case connection','Mitral valve disease → left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure rises → capillary hydrostatic pressure rises → pulmonary edema. Contrast with sepsis/inflammation (permeability), hypoalbuminemia (oncotic), and lymphatic blockage (drainage).']
 ]},
 hemostasis1:{title:'11 · Hemostasis I',source:'Instructor Lecture 11 · 41 slides',summary:'Vasoconstriction → platelet plug → fibrin stabilization; intrinsic/extrinsic/common coagulation.',sections:[
 ['Four steps','1. Transient vasoconstriction (endothelin and local reflex); 2. primary hemostasis (platelet adhesion, activation and aggregation); 3. secondary hemostasis (coagulation cascade/fibrin); 4. remodeling and eventual clot removal.'],
 ['Platelet adhesion','Endothelial disruption exposes subendothelial collagen. von Willebrand factor (vWF) bridges collagen to platelet GPIb receptors; adhesion then promotes platelet activation and shape change.'],
 ['Activation and aggregation','Activated platelets release ADP and thromboxane A2 and display GPIIb/IIIa, which binds fibrinogen to bridge adjacent platelets. Primary plug is fast but unstable without fibrin.'],
 ['Coagulation pathways','Extrinsic: tissue factor + VII → X. Intrinsic contact pathway: XII → XI → IX with VIII → X. Common: X + V converts II (prothrombin) to IIa (thrombin); thrombin converts I (fibrinogen) to fibrin; XIII crosslinks fibrin. This is the simplified teaching cascade; in vivo coagulation is cell-surface driven.'],
 ['Vitamin K and pathology link','Vitamin K-dependent coagulation factors are II, VII, IX and X (proteins C and S also vitamin K dependent). Rodenticide anticoagulants impair the vitamin K cycle and predispose to severe internal bleeding.'],
 ['Why fibrin matters','Platelets provide the initial temporary seal; thrombin-generated fibrin strengthens the plug. Platelet failure favors superficial mucosal bleeding; coagulation-factor failure often causes deeper bleeding or cavity hemorrhage.']
 ]},
 hemostasis2:{title:'12 · Hemostasis II',source:'Instructor Lecture 12 · 21 slides',summary:'Virchow triad; healthy versus injured endothelium; fibrinolysis and coagulation inhibitors.',sections:[
 ['Resting vs injured endothelium','Intact endothelium is antiplatelet, anticoagulant and fibrinolytic. Injury exposes collagen and favors platelet adhesion, coagulation and thrombus formation.'],
 ['Virchow triad','THREE contributors to pathological thrombosis: endothelial injury, abnormal flow (stasis or turbulence), and hypercoagulability. They may coexist; stasis particularly favors venous thrombi.'],
 ['Endothelial antiplatelet actions','Prostacyclin (PGI2), nitric oxide (NO), and ADP-metabolizing enzymes discourage platelet activation away from the lesion.'],
 ['Natural anticoagulants','Antithrombin inhibits thrombin (IIa) and factor Xa; endothelial heparan sulfate augments its activity. Thrombomodulin-associated protein C/S pathways and tissue factor pathway inhibitor limit excess coagulation.'],
 ['Fibrinolysis — remove fibrin','Endothelial tPA and urokinase activate plasminogen → plasmin, which digests fibrin into fibrin degradation products. PAI-1 inhibits plasminogen activators; alpha-2 antiplasmin limits free plasmin. D-dimers reflect breakdown of crosslinked fibrin but are not specific for DIC.'],
 ['Thrombosis vs fibrinolysis','Thrombosis = inappropriate intravascular thrombus formation. Fibrinolysis = regulated breakdown of fibrin; thrombolysis refers to dissolution of a thrombus. Balance prevents both persistent bleeding and uncontrolled clot extension.']
 ]},
 hemorrhage:{title:'13 · Hemorrhage & Thrombosis',source:'Instructor Lecture 13 · 41 slides',summary:'Gross bleeding patterns; antemortem thrombus versus postmortem clot; outcomes.',sections:[
 ['Two mechanisms of hemorrhage','Per rhexis: rupture/break in vessel wall. Per diapedesis: leakage of erythrocytes across an altered vessel wall without a macroscopic tear.'],
 ['Name the gross hemorrhage','Petechiae ~1–2 mm; purpura intermediate; ecchymoses larger blotches; paintbrush linear hemorrhage; suffusion sheet-like spread along a tissue plane; hematoma localized collection in tissue. Hemo-thorax/pericardium/peritoneum/arthrosis names cavity blood.'],
 ['Mechanisms and etiologies','Trauma, vasculitis/endothelial injury, thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction, coagulation factor deficiency, severe liver disease, DIC and anticoagulant rodenticide exposure cause overlapping patterns. Location and history narrow the cause.'],
 ['Bleeding consequences','Clinical impact depends on amount, rate, and location. Fast major loss risks hypovolemic shock; slow hemorrhage can trigger increased erythropoiesis and compensation. Even smaller hemorrhage in brain or pericardium may be lethal.'],
 ['Thrombi: arterial vs venous','Arterial thrombi often develop with turbulence/endothelial disruption and are platelet/fibrin rich; venous thrombi often form with stasis, contain more entrapped erythrocytes and tend to obstruct venous flow.'],
 ['Antemortem thrombus versus postmortem clot','A true thrombus is attached to a vessel wall and often has layered architecture; a postmortem clot is typically smooth, gelatinous, nonadherent and may separate into red cruor and yellow chicken-fat portions. Avoid diagnosing thrombosis from color alone.'],
 ['Possible thrombus fates','Propagation (extension), embolization (detachment), dissolution via fibrinolysis, and organization with possible recanalization. Downstream ischemia and infarction depend on vessel and collateral blood flow.'],
 ['Instructor examples','Guinea pig vitamin C deficiency → weak collagen and vascular fragility/hemarthrosis; canine anticoagulant rodenticide → bleeding due to loss of vitamin K-dependent factors; hemopericardium → possible cardiac tamponade.']
 ]},
 ischemia:{title:'14 · Embolism, Ischemia & Shock',source:'Instructor Lecture 14 · 43 slides',summary:'Embolic types; acute/chronic infarcts; shock mechanisms and compensations.',sections:[
 ['Ischemia vs hypoxia','Hypoxia is oxygen deficiency. Ischemia is insufficient perfusion, reducing oxygen AND substrate delivery and waste clearance; prolonged ischemia often causes infarction.'],
 ['Types of emboli','Dislodged thrombus (most common); septic bacterial aggregates; parasitic fragments; neoplastic cells; fibrocartilage from nucleus pulposus; fat from marrow trauma; air. Their sites of arrest depend on the vascular route.'],
 ['Routes and specimens','Venous emboli travel through right heart into pulmonary arteries. Systemic arterial emboli travel to tissues via left heart. Septic emboli may seed kidney glomeruli; treated canine heartworm can cause pulmonary verminous thromboemboli.'],
 ['Infarct appearance','Acute infarct may be dark red to pale and sharply demarcated without major tissue contraction. Chronic infarct often becomes pale, firm and depressed/shrunken as necrotic tissue is replaced by fibrous scar.'],
 ['White/red infarcts and histology','Solid end-arterial organs often develop pale infarcts, whereas dual circulation or venous obstruction can create red hemorrhagic infarcts. Most non-CNS ischemic infarcts have coagulative necrosis; CNS ischemia generally causes liquefaction/malacia.'],
 ['Venous obstruction','Strangulating/twisting bowel can collapse thin-walled veins first, while arterial inflow persists for a time. Severe congestion, hemorrhage, hypoxia, and eventually necrosis follow.'],
 ['Shock — mechanism','Systemic hypoperfusion leads to inadequate tissue oxygen delivery and cellular energy failure. Distinguish hypovolemic (low circulating volume), cardiogenic (pump failure), and distributive causes such as septic/anaphylactic shock; evaluate case physiology, not just a single blood pressure.'],
 ['Compensation and decompensation','Early sympathetic vasoconstriction/tachycardia and fluid-retaining responses help preserve vital perfusion. Prolonged hypoperfusion causes lactate accumulation, endothelial injury, organ failure and potentially irreversible shock.']
 ]},
 vascular:{title:'15 · Vascular Disorders',source:'Instructor Lecture 15 · 38 slides',summary:'Vessel microanatomy; rupture, aneurysm, arteriosclerosis, vasculitis and lymphatics.',sections:[
 ['Vessel structure and purpose','Arteries have muscular/elastic walls for high pressure; veins are thin-walled low-pressure reservoirs; lymphatics return interstitial fluid. Endothelium regulates exchange, inflammation, angiogenesis and hemostasis.'],
 ['Capillary types','Continuous (brain, lung, muscle); fenestrated (renal glomeruli, intestinal villi, endocrine organs); discontinuous/sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow). Structure controls what crosses the barrier.'],
 ['Rupture and aneurysm','Vessel rupture causes hemorrhage; dissecting aneurysm involves blood dissecting into a damaged vessel wall. Slide examples include equine aortic and carotid rupture and uterine artery rupture in multiparous mares.'],
 ['Arteriosclerosis versus atherosclerosis','Arteriosclerosis broadly means arterial wall thickening/hardening and may include mineralization. Atherosclerosis involves lipid-rich intimal plaques with inflammation and fibrosis; lecture mentions primates, pigs, birds and dyslipidemia/endocrine conditions in dogs.'],
 ['Vasculitis cases','Vascular inflammation can cause leakage, thrombosis, ischemia and cutaneous infarcts. Recognize diamond skin disease in pigs, mulberry heart disease in pigs, and FIP-associated vascular disease in cats as DISTINCT etiologic settings.'],
 ['Parasite-associated vessels','Dirofilaria immitis in dogs can cause pulmonary arteritis, thromboembolism and cardiopulmonary dysfunction. Strongylus vulgaris larval migration can cause cranial mesenteric arteritis in horses.'],
 ['Lymphatic lesions','Lymphangiectasia is abnormal lymphatic dilation, particularly intestinal and potentially associated with protein-losing enteropathy; lymphangitis is lymphatic inflammation. Contrast with passive interstitial edema.']
 ]},
 cases16:{title:'16 · Practice Cases',source:'Instructor Lecture 16 · 58 slides',summary:'Describe first, construct morphologic diagnoses and explain mechanisms from case images.',sections:[
 ['Necropsy reasoning flow','Describe tissue/site, count, distribution, size, shape, color, texture and special features → morphologic diagnosis (organ, process, modifiers, distribution, chronicity, severity) → pathogenesis → supported etiology.'],
 ['Case 1 — bovine skin and subcutis','Inflamed, hemorrhagic, edematous subcutis in a cow with pneumonia: assess vasculature and increased microvascular permeability; do not reduce the interpretation to “red = hyperemia” without recognizing hemorrhage.'],
 ['Case 2 — dog lung with mitral valve disease','Foamy fluid and alveolar edema in a dog with left heart failure: mitral disease → pulmonary venous pressure increases → hydrostatic filtration exceeds drainage → edema. Contrast cardiogenic edema with inflammation.'],
 ['Case 3 — steroid-treated dog liver','Pale enlarged liver with swollen, cleared hepatocytes: link chronic glucocorticoid exposure to glycogen-type vacuolar hepatopathy, and separate lipid vacuoles from glycogen in histology.'],
 ['Case 4 — dog spleen','Sharply demarcated splenic infarcts require lesion distribution, timing and vascular supply reasoning; coagulative necrosis is the typical microscopic consequence.'],
 ['Case 5 — equine intestine','Dark, congested intestinal segment with strangulation: venous obstruction can precede arterial collapse; distinguish reversible venous infarction/congestion from established tissue necrosis using histology and chronology.'],
 ['Practice rule','The instructor states that individual cases are supplemental; foundational concepts and lesion recognition, rather than memorizing the exact cases, are the exam priority.']
 ]}
});
